<header>
  <h1>Glossary</h1>
  <p>What the terms on the SupplyChain Sentinel dashboard actually mean. <a href="index.html">&larr; Back to dashboard</a></p>
</header>

<main>
  <section>
    <h2>Entities (the data model)</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Policy Event <code>PolicyEvent</code></dt>
      <dd>
        One government/news action about pharma trade — a tariff change, an export restriction, a
        regulatory probe. Extracted by the <strong>Policy Agent</strong> from a raw government notice
        or news item, filtered for actual pharma relevance (most raw pulls are noise and get discarded).
        Has a <em>country</em>, <em>policy type</em>, <em>affected product</em>, <em>date</em>,
        <em>severity</em>, and a source citation.
        <span class="example">e.g. a Federal Register notice cutting US tariffs on EU-origin generic drugs</span>
      </dd>

      <dt>Filing Mention <code>FilingMention</code></dt>
      <dd>
        One disclosed dependency from a company's own SEC 10-K — "we manufacture in Ireland," "we depend
        on China-based API suppliers." Extracted by the <strong>Filing Agent</strong> from cached filing
        excerpts, filtered to exclude generic boilerplate and incidental country mentions (e.g. "we sell
        our drug in Japan" does NOT count — that's a market, not a sourcing/manufacturing dependency).
        <span class="example">e.g. AbbVie's 10-K listing manufacturing sites in Ireland</span>
      </dd>

      <dt>Graph Link <code>GraphLink</code></dt>
      <dd>
        A confirmed connection between one Policy Event and one Filing Mention, found because they share
        a country (and optionally a commodity). This is the actual cross-referencing step — checking
        whether a policy notice and a company's own words are talking about the same place. Plain country
        string matching, done in <code>src/graph.py</code>, no LLM call.
      </dd>

      <dt>Alert Card</dt>
      <dd>
        The final, human-readable output for one Graph Link: a title, a severity, and a plain-English
        explanation that must cite both the policy source and the filing source. One more LLM call per
        link, given only the specific policy snippet and filing excerpt (grounding both facts and, per
        the prompt, forbidding invented specifics not in that text).
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Gap vs. Opportunity</h2>
    <p style="color:#334155; font-size:14px;">
      Neither side of a Graph Link is "more correct" than the other — a link needs BOTH a matching
      Policy Event AND a matching Filing Mention. Either side can be the one that's missing:
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Gap</dt>
      <dd>
        A Filing Mention with <strong>no matching Policy Event</strong>. A company disclosed a real
        dependency in its own 10-K, but nothing on the policy side confirms or contextualizes it yet.
        <span class="example">e.g. Eli Lilly's 10-K states it depends on China-based API suppliers, but this run's
        policy data pull has no China-specific tariff notice to link it to</span>
      </dd>
      <dt>Opportunity</dt>
      <dd>
        A Policy Event with <strong>no matching Filing Mention</strong>. A real policy action happened,
        but no covered company's filing has been tied to it yet — worth a closer read of that company's
        filing, or it may mean none of the companies tracked in this segment are actually exposed.
        <span class="example">e.g. a US-Switzerland tariff notice exists, but no tracked company's filing
        names Switzerland as a manufacturing/sourcing location this run</span>
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Graph terms</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Catalyst</dt>
      <dd>
        The graph's leftmost node — a Policy Event's short, human-readable <code>headline</code> (e.g.
        "US-EU Framework Tariff Cut on Generic Drugs"), generated by the Policy Agent specifically so a
        reader isn't stuck decoding a raw <code>country / policy_type</code> pair.
      </dd>
      <dt>Shared country / EU bloc matching</dt>
      <dd>
        The country a Policy Event and a Filing Mention have in common — the actual condition
        <code>src/graph.py</code> checks to form a link. Some government notices describe an EU-wide
        action ("products of the European Union") without naming one specific member state; those are
        recorded as country <code>"European Union"</code> and matched against a Filing Mention naming
        <em>any</em> EU member (see <code>EU_MEMBERS</code> in <code>src/graph.py</code>), with the link's
        displayed "shared country" resolved to that specific member state, not the literal bloc name.
      </dd>
      <dt>hop_count / confidence</dt>
      <dd>
        <code>hop_count: 1</code> (confidence 0.9) means the Policy Event and Filing Mention share both
        country AND commodity. <code>hop_count: 2</code> (confidence 0.6) means they share only the
        country. Commodity-only matches (same product, different country) are deliberately excluded from
        the graph entirely — too weak a signal at this scope.
      </dd>
      <dt>Detailed vs. Simplified view</dt>
      <dd>
        Detailed shows all four columns: Catalyst &rarr; Policy Scope (the event's raw country/commodity)
        &rarr; Shared Country &rarr; Company. Simplified collapses the middle two into one hop: Catalyst
        &rarr; Country &rarr; Company.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Pipeline / evaluation terms</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Baseline</dt>
      <dd>
        A single direct LLM prompt over the same raw cached text (no graph, no forced citations, no
        structure) — the comparison point the two-agent pipeline has to beat. Generated once
        (<code>data/baseline_output.json</code>) and frozen; never regenerated after the Phase 6
        evaluation runs against it, so the comparison stays reproducible.
      </dd>
      <dt>Trajectory</dt>
      <dd>
        A saved log of one real agent call: the exact system prompt, user prompt, raw model response, and
        parsed output, written to <code>trajectories/</code> during the actual pipeline run — not
        reconstructed afterward.
      </dd>
      <dt>Golden case</dt>
      <dd>
        One hand-labeled test case in <code>eval/golden_events_pharma.yaml</code> — a specific
        company/country pair with a known correct answer (should the pipeline find a link, or correctly
        not find one), written from manually reviewing the cached raw data.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
</main>

